window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][17] = "<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Re-record the narration of 40 courses</td>\n<td>Language quality</td>\n<td>A quarter of the budget; does not touch the main cause</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Downloads over Wi-Fi, a low-data video version (about 30 MB a lesson) and profiles for shared phones</td>\n<td>Data cost, storage and shared use</td>\n<td>About six weeks of engineering; slightly lower video quality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Cut the price</td>\n<td>Affordability of the subscription</td>\n<td>Revenue; does not change the data cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Partner with local shops that have Wi-Fi to offer lesson downloads</td>\n<td>Distribution and trust</td>\n<td>Partner management; slower to scale</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B directly addresses the observed constraints and gives up some video quality. D builds on what households already do and brings a trusted face to the product, at the price of operations. A addresses the team's belief and the smallest share of the data. C is a pricing move that leaves the data constraint in place.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Build B, and pilot D with ten shops. Primary metric: the share of installs that are active learners in week four (baseline 14%, invented), which avoids counting the first-week drop twice. Input: lessons completed per active learner and megabytes used per lesson. Guardrails: crash rate and the share of lessons that fail to start. Counter metric: the share of users who switch profiles more than three times in a session, which shows whether sharing is being handled or made awkward.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario, inactivity after the first week fell from 70% to 52%, and the share of installs that were active in week four rose from 14% to 26% (invented). Learners using downloads completed more lessons per week than streamers, and the shops in the pilot brought in 15% of new installs (invented). The narration budget was kept for later use on the five courses that the data showed learners abandoned for content reasons.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If activity had not moved after the downloads shipped, the team would have returned to the households to learn what else stopped learners, perhaps the evening availability of the phone. If profiles had confused users, they would have moved to a simple code on each learner's first screen. If the shop pilot had struggled, a carrier partnership would have been the next test. The lesson is the method: when your own belief gives the answer and your fieldwork gives another, trust the fieldwork, and check the constraints of the user's situation before the quality of the content.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c09-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C09-02 · Kenya, 2007: M-Pesa and the product that changed course · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): a first-person account by the pilot's leaders, Nick Hughes and Susie Lonie, &quot;M-PESA: Mobile Money for the 'Unbanked': Turning Cellphones into 24-Hour Tellers in Kenya,&quot; <em>Innovations: Technology, Governance, Globalization</em> 2(1-2), 63 to 81 (MIT Press, 2007; doi 10.1162/itgg.2007.2.1-2.63), read in full (primary); an overview by Financial Sector Deepening Kenya (a development-sector organization, 2009); the World Bank working paper &quot;Mobile Payments Go Viral: M-PESA in Kenya&quot; (2010); two NBER working papers on M-Pesa (&quot;Mobile Money: The Economics of M-PESA&quot; and &quot;Mobile Banking: The Impact of M-Pesa in Kenya&quot;), which cite Safaricom's figures; and a case summary by the Centre for Public Impact. The analysis is the author's. This is a design analysis from public information and does not claim knowledge of internal decisions beyond the published accounts. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> Safaricom, Kenya's largest mobile operator and partly owned by Vodafone, developed a mobile-money service, with Vodafone owning, hosting and developing the platform, with funding support from the UK's Department for International Development (a matched grant from its Financial Deepening Challenge Fund of nearly £1 million, matched by Vodafone), in partnership with a microfinance institution, Faulu Kenya, and a commercial bank, the Commercial Bank of Africa, which held the customers' money <code>[V: Hughes and Lonie, 2007]</code>. Its first concept was a way for microfinance borrowers to receive and repay loans using the operator's network of airtime resellers as agents, which would cut the cost of handling cash for lenders. During pilot use in and around Nairobi, customers were also sending money to one another. The pilot began on 11 October 2005 with eight agent stores and nearly 500 clients. Its leaders concluded that a mass-market launch with a microfinance institution would be too complex, and built the launch around the proposition &quot;Send Money Home&quot;, with three features: cash deposit and withdrawal at agents, person-to-person transfer and prepaid airtime. Safaricom launched it commercially in March 2007 <code>[V: Hughes and Lonie, 2007]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> As documented or reported: in 2007 there were no regulations that applied to a mobile-money service, so the Central Bank of Kenya had to be engaged: after product demonstrations, documentation and a formal legal opinion it confirmed that it had no objection, and the service launched ten days after receiving that letter (Hughes and Lonie). The service needed places where people could turn cash into electronic value and back, and agents hesitated at first to pay out cash, which the project leaders say they solved by giving agents separate cash floats and head-office reassurance (Hughes and Lonie). The service had to work on the basic handsets customers already had, since smartphones were not abundant in Kenya, so the designers chose text messages and a menu on the SIM card (Hughes and Lonie). Safaricom's subscriber base had grown from a few thousand five years earlier to about 5 million by the pilot, and its several hundred airtime dealer outlets became the first agents, which reduced the cost of reaching customers (Hughes and Lonie).</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> From the pilot, as the leaders' account describes: loan repayment was the intended use, and person-to-person transfers emerged in practice. From the launch: over 20,000 customers registered in the first month, ahead of expectation (Hughes and Lonie). Safaricom's own figures, as cited in the World Bank and NBER papers, show over 1.1 million registered customers within eight months of the March 2007 launch and about 8.5 million by September 2009; the World Bank paper gives the internal launch target as about 1 million customers within a year, (Vodafone's own 2017 account by Michael Joseph, who led Safaricom at the launch, says the original business plan aimed for 350,000 customers at the end of the first year and that he pushed the team to aim for a million, and reports 1.2 million customers at the end of the first year; other sources give higher first-year counts, so figures for the first twelve months vary by source) <code>[V: World Bank working paper, 2010; NBER working papers; Vodafone, 6 March 2017]</code>. Agent locations grew from about 450 in mid-2007 to over 18,000 by April 2010, and the 2009 FinAccess survey found that 40% of Kenyan adults had used the service <code>[V: NBER working papers; Financial Sector Deepening Kenya]</code>; the Centre for Public Impact reports about 40,000 agent outlets in April 2016, while Safaricom's own ten-year release counts more than 101,000 agents at the end of March 2016, so agent counts differ by what is counted <code>[V: Centre for Public Impact; Safaricom, 'Kenya Marks 10 Years of M-PESA']</code>. The service was marketed around sending money home (Financial Sector Deepening Kenya).</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> <em>The author's reading.</em> The designers' original problem was the cost of handling cash for microfinance repayment. The behavior in the pilot showed a different pain point: moving money to relatives across distance, which the sources describe as common, depended on informal channels (sending cash with someone travelling by bus, or costly formal services such as Western Union with few outlets in rural areas) and was costly, slow or risky <code>[V: Hughes and Lonie, 2007]</code>. As an observable pain point: &quot;A person who wants to send money to a relative in another town must carry it, send it with someone, or travel.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's analysis.</em> Read through this chapter's frameworks, the case is a clear example of discovery without an existing market (C09.2): the team's plan was not confirmed by surveys but by what people did with the pilot. The team observed a workaround (people using the service to send money to each other) and followed it. The binding constraints from the stack were payments (cash was the endpoint, and the system had to turn cash into value and back), trust (a person gives cash to a stranger and expects a relative to be able to collect it) and devices (the service ran on the phones customers had). The design answered each: the agent network provided cash in and out, and it was a trust signal because the agents were shopkeepers customers already knew. The problem at the start (agents not wanting to hold cash) was solved with a change to the operator's design of floats, which is the kind of operational detail that a PM should expect to own. The distribution advantage was the operator's existing subscriber base and airtime-reseller network, which turned a costly step (reaching people and places) into something nearly pre-built. Regulation appears as a prerequisite that was handled by engagement and not by waiting.</p>\n<p>The case also carries a caution. It is a survivor: the factors that mattered (an operator with a large share of subscribers, a regulator willing to let a pilot proceed, a dense network of small shops, a real remittance need) were particular to Kenya at that time. The same design in a market without them could have failed, and this case does not show that it would transfer.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>The author's reconstruction of choices a team could have faced; not a record of the company's deliberations.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Launch as designed, a loan disbursement and repayment tool for microfinance</td>\n<td>The original plan and the partner's need</td>\n<td>Ignores the behavior seen in the pilot; narrow use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Re-target to person-to-person transfers, using the same agents</td>\n<td>The need observed in practice, reach through the existing network</td>\n<td>New marketing and risk; regulatory ambiguity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Build a bank-style account requiring branches</td>\n<td>Familiar financial product</td>\n<td>Branches are the bottleneck the service was meant to avoid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Wait for regulation before launch</td>\n<td>Legal certainty</td>\n<td>Delay, and the opportunity to learn from live use</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B follows the evidence and uses the strongest asset (the agent network), at the cost of regulatory uncertainty and of operating a service for which the rules did not yet exist. A would have been simpler and narrower, C leaned on the bottleneck, and D traded learning for certainty.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, Safaricom changed the service from a loan-repayment tool to a general money-transfer service and launched it commercially in March 2007 <code>[V: Hughes and Lonie, 2007]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Reported: strong early adoption, over 1.1 million registered customers within eight months, over 18,000 agent locations by April 2010, use by 40% of Kenyan adults by the 2009 FinAccess survey, and additional services built on the platform <code>[V: NBER working papers; Financial Sector Deepening Kenya]</code>. Whether the success was caused by the design, by the operator's reach, by the regulatory stance, or by timing is a separate question that this case does not settle.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>Inference.</em> Had the team treated the pilot's person-to-person use as misuse and held to the loan product, the service might have stayed small. Had agents refused to hold cash, the network would have stalled, which is why the float fix mattered. Had the regulator objected, the launch could have been delayed or changed in form. The method to carry away: observe what people do with a first version, follow the workaround, build the distribution and trust the product needs (not only the interface), and treat the rules as part of the design.</p>\n<span id=\"c09-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Seven questions: two Beginner, two Intermediate, three Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code>; every figure is an assumption. Each is written against the six behaviors in section 1 and uses the spine in FW-C09-01. The answers are fuller than the first pass of a real round; a spoken version keeps the same order with fewer words. Where a real company or country is the setting, the answer reasons from user needs and public information and states no internal fact. Market facts that may change carry verify marks.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c09-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C09-01 · Beginner · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A messaging app wants to grow in a country where many users have phones with very little free storage. What do you change?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Make the app smaller,&quot; with no reasoning about who is affected or how storage is lost.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies how the storage problem shows up for users, chooses a segment, offers different kinds of move and recommends one with a metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that received media, not the app, is the main consumer of storage, protects users who want to keep media, links storage to data cost, and states what would change the plan.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll state assumptions first, since I know this only in general terms: many phones have under about 1 GB of free storage, and these users are a sizable share of the target market. I'd verify that with device data on free storage at install and at uninstall.</p>\n<p><em>Segment.</em> Users on phones with little free space. I choose them over users with ample storage because they are the ones losing the product. I give up users on high-end phones, who have no problem.</p>\n<p><em>Problem.</em> A user's phone warns that storage is nearly full, and the easiest thing to delete is the largest app, or its media. The app is the first to go. Received photos and videos from large groups fill space quickly, and re-installing costs data. The workaround is the evidence: deleting the app and reinstalling later, or deleting media by hand.</p>\n<p><em>The four moves.</em> Fix: make the app and its updates smaller (updates that send only what changed). Add: a storage manager that shows what uses space by chat and clears it in one tap. Remove: stop automatically downloading video over mobile data by default. Reposition: offer a &quot;lite&quot; version. I recommend the Add and the Remove together: a storage manager and a default that downloads media on request, which saves storage and data cost at once. The Fix is useful and smaller in effect, since the app is a minor share of what fills the phone.</p>\n<p><em>Constraint scan.</em> Legacy users: people who like to keep every photo must be able to, so media they star is never cleared automatically, and nothing is deleted without a clear choice. Ecosystem: media removed from the phone should remain retrievable from the sender or the cloud where the product offers it, so nothing is lost for good.</p>\n<p><em>Size.</em> Assume 100,000,000 users and 30% storage-constrained: 30,000,000. Monthly uninstall rate is 12% for them against 4% for others (invented). If the change cuts the excess in half (4 points), that is 1,200,000 fewer uninstalls a month; with a 30% haircut, 840,000.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: 30-day retention among storage-constrained users. Input: the share who open the storage manager. Guardrails: media delivery success and the rate of &quot;media not found&quot; reports. Counter metric: the rate at which users complain about lost media, which would show that the default is too aggressive. I'd change my mind if the uninstall data showed that few exits followed a storage warning, in which case the cause is elsewhere.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if users say they want to keep all received media on the phone? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then the design should give them control, not decide for them. Deleting automatically would break a real need, so I would make a storage view that shows the largest chats and media by size, offer suggestions such as media older than 90 days that is already backed up, and let the user choose what to clear. I would keep a one-tap restore from a cloud copy or the sender where it is available. The measures are the share of low-storage users who free space with the tool and, as a guardrail, the rate of complaints about lost media. The default should protect media, and the tool should make space cheap to recover.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How does storage relate to data cost, and how would you use that? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Both are scarce for these users, and media is the main driver of each. Auto-downloading photos and videos uses data and storage at once. I would let the user choose automatic download by network type (Wi-Fi only, always, never), show an estimate of data used by media each week, and compress by default with a choice of quality. I would measure data used per active user and storage freed, with message delivery success as the guardrail. If many users choose Wi-Fi only, that tells me the default should change for this market.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c09-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C09-02 · Beginner · product design · DI</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> You have two weeks and a small budget to learn whether people in a market you have never visited would use a savings product. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Run an online survey and ask whether people would use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Studies today's workarounds, uses local researchers, observes behavior, and sets a decision rule before starting.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Corrects for biases specific to the setting, tests with a commitment and not an opinion, spots the licensing issue in handling people's money, and says what would stop the project.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd use the discovery plan (FW-C09-04). The decision this informs is whether to build a savings product for this market and for whom. I'd write the rule before I start: proceed if there is a widespread workaround that costs people something real, and if enough people show a behavior and not only an opinion.</p>\n<p><em>Week 1: workarounds, proxies and fieldwork.</em> I'd hire a local researcher, since they know the language and norms and will be trusted. I'd expect informal savings (groups that pool money, a shopkeeper or relative who holds cash), and I'd ask about the last time a person set money aside, where it went and what went wrong. I'd also look at proxies: how people top up their phones tells me how they handle small sums, and how they send money tells me about trust and distance. I'd visit three places (a market, a workplace, a neighborhood), speak to about 12 people, and watch what they do, not only what they say.</p>\n<p><em>Week 2: a cheap behavioral test.</em> A concierge test: with a licensed partner, because holding other people's money without a license is not something I can do informally, a local agent collects small deposits from 20 people for four weeks and records them in a notebook, with a simple receipt in the local language. The signal is deposits made, not interest expressed.</p>\n<p><em>Biases to correct.</em> Politeness: people may say they would use it to please a visitor, which is why I ask about behavior. Interviewer effects: a local researcher who is not visibly senior. Sampling: an online survey or a phone survey would miss people without a phone, so I'd reach people in person. Translation: I'd have the questions translated and translated back.</p>\n<p><em>Decision rule.</em> Proceed if at least 12 of the 20 deposit in at least three of the four weeks (invented), and if interviews show that today's workaround costs people through fees, loss or risk. Stop or reshape if people save but prefer the informal route for reasons of trust that a product cannot change. What I would not conclude: that the market &quot;is not ready.&quot; If the test fails I'd find the reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you correct for biases specific to a market you have never visited? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would hire a local researcher and let them shape the plan, because they know the language, the norms and the social dynamics of an interview. Typical biases here: respondents telling a foreigner what they think is polite (courtesy bias), a sample drawn from the people who are easiest to reach, who tend to be more educated and urban, and questions that do not translate. I would interview in the local language, include people from different incomes and from outside the capital, ask about what they did last month and not what they would do, and compare what they say with what the proxies show.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What commitment test would you use, and what would stop the project? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;A test that asks for something real, such as a pilot in which 30 people deposit a small amount of their own money through an existing local mechanism and a local partner holds it, with consent and with the licensing question checked first. Proceed if at least 40% of invited people deposit and at least half make a second deposit within a month; stop if under 15%, or if the license route is closed or would take longer than the project can support. Handling people's money is regulated, so legal advice comes before any live money, not after.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c09-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C09-03 · Intermediate · product design · PS · Google</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design a feature for a video app for people whose connections are unreliable and whose data is expensive.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A lower-quality mode.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names the binding constraint, ranks the pain points, offers different kinds of solution, and handles storage and data use.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Considers the effect on the people who make the videos, uses offline behavior as the primary design, protects users who want autoplay and quality, and sets guardrails on data use and storage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll assume the existing app on mid-range phones, and a goal of more watching by people who now watch little or not at all because of cost and connection quality. Role: a core product that people choose for entertainment and learning. I know this only from public information.</p>\n<p><em>Segment.</em> Viewers on a monthly data cap, on connections that drop, who commute or live where signal is poor. I give up viewers on home Wi-Fi, who have no issue.</p>\n<p><em>Constraint stack.</em> Connectivity binds: cost and reliability. Devices follow: storage is limited, so downloads must be managed. Shared use and payment are secondary.</p>\n<p><em>Pain points.</em> (1) A video buffers or fails mid-way and the data used is lost. (2) Autoplay and high default quality use the plan without the user choosing to. (3) The user cannot tell how much data a video will use. (4) Storage fills with downloads. I choose (1) to (3), which together are the data problem.</p>\n<p><em>Solutions.</em> Assist: show the data used per hour and an estimate before playing. Automate: smart downloads of videos that the user is likely to watch, fetched on Wi-Fi overnight. Reframe: treat Wi-Fi as the place where the library is filled and mobile data as the exception. Remove: turn autoplay off on mobile data by default. Connect: let a friend share a downloaded video directly between phones. I recommend smart downloads with the data display and autoplay off on mobile data. They address the cause (data use at the wrong moment and place) and use an existing habit.</p>\n<p><em>Constraint scan.</em> Legacy users: those who like autoplay keep a setting, and quality stays high on Wi-Fi. Ecosystem: downloaded videos must expire and respect rights, and people who make videos earn from views, so offline views have to be counted and credited. Storage: downloads are capped and cleared with a simple rule, such as removing the oldest watched ones first.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: watch time per month among capped users. Input: the share of watch time served from downloads. Guardrails: megabytes used per watched hour, and the share of downloads that expire unwatched, which measures waste. Counter metric: uninstalls linked to a full storage warning. I'd change my mind if capped users said their main problem was the cost of the plan itself, in which case a carrier partnership is the first move.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that prompts at Google often concern its products for users with limited connectivity <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or product behavior is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How does the design affect the people who make the videos? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Creators lose views if low-bandwidth viewers get a lower-quality default or cannot find their videos offline. I would keep the original upload quality available, give creators a view of how many viewers watched in a data-saving mode, and make sure that offline downloads still count as views and credit the creator. A guardrail is the change in view counts and watch time for creators in these markets. If the feature raises viewing by new users and lowers creators' earnings, it needs a fix before it expands, since creators are the supply side of the product.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you protect users who want autoplay and full quality? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By making data-saving a choice with a clear default for people on a cap, not a loss for everyone. The setting would offer Standard, Data saver and Offline-first, with an estimate of data per hour for each, and the user can change it at any time. For someone on home Wi-Fi the app should not degrade the picture. I would measure watch time and the share of users who switch settings, and set guardrails on data used per session and on storage used by downloads, with an easy way to clear them.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c09-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C09-04 · Intermediate · product design · PS · Stripe</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A payments company wants to help small online merchants in a new country. How do you decide what to build first?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Support the local currency and the most popular cards.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Ranks payment methods by the share of payments they carry in the target segment, considers the licensing lead time, and builds in phases.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats licensing, verification and data storage as part of the product, finds the revenue model the market allows, and says how to learn quickly while the license is pending.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll assume a company with a payments platform elsewhere, and a goal of a first cohort of merchants taking payments reliably. Country details are assumptions: I'd verify them with public data on payment methods and with interviews.</p>\n<p><em>Segment.</em> Small merchants who sell through social media and chat and take orders without a website, over established stores that already integrate gateways. They have the most pain and the least service. I give up the developers and stores where our existing product fits, and I'd serve them with the same platform later.</p>\n<p><em>What the market uses.</em> Assume, for the target segment's sales by value (invented): bank transfers through a local QR system 55%, cash on delivery 30%, cards 15%. These numbers drive the ranking.</p>\n<p><em>Options.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Share of target sales covered</th>\n<th>Time to market</th>\n<th>Risk</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Cards first (our existing capability)</td>\n<td>15%</td>\n<td>Short: about two months</td>\n<td>Low, and a small market</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. The local bank-transfer method</td>\n<td>55%</td>\n<td>Longer: about six months, with licensing</td>\n<td>Medium: regulation and a new integration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Cash-on-delivery support and reconciliation</td>\n<td>30%</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium: the cash is collected by a third party</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend B as the main build, since it carries the most sales, with the licensing and verification work started in week one because it has the longest lead time. In parallel I'd launch A to a small group of merchants who also sell abroad, to learn about onboarding, settlement and support, which would otherwise wait six months. C comes next, built as a reconciliation tool that matches courier-collected cash to orders. This gives up an early broad launch.</p>\n<p><em>Beyond methods.</em> Merchant verification in local terms (a tiered process with lower limits at first, so honest sellers without formal papers are not excluded), data storage in the country if the rules require it, settlement timing in local business days, and a dashboard in the local language. Revenue: I'd check whether the local method carries a merchant fee by policy; if it does not, I'd price the software (reconciliation, invoices, reports) and earn on the minority of payments that allow a fee <code>[V: merchant-fee FAQs of 15 September 2026: a fee applies from 15 October 2026 to merchant payments above 2,000 rupees]</code>.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of target merchants able to accept their most-used method, and the share of payment attempts that succeed, by method. Input: time from sign-up to first payment. Guardrails: fraud and dispute rates, and settlement delay. Counter metric: the share of merchants who keep using their old method in parallel after three months, which shows a gap. I'd change my mind if interviews showed that cash on delivery was the dominant pain, in which case C would move first.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Stripe is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Stripe publishes documentation on local payment methods and <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that prompts ask how to prioritize payment methods and compliance in a new country <code>[V: Stripe docs, Supported payment methods; the prompt descriptions remain candidate-reported]</code>. No Stripe data or roadmap is claimed.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you learn quickly while the license is pending? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By separating what needs the license from what does not. Before approval I can run merchant interviews, build the onboarding and verification flow in a sandbox, test the dashboard and reporting with merchants using test money, and sign a banking or licensed partner who can process live payments under their license. I would write the plan with the regulator's timeline in view and set a decision date: if the license route takes more than, say, 12 months, move to the partner model for launch. The aim is to have a tested product and a first cohort ready the day the license arrives.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you find a revenue model the market allows? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By checking what fees are permitted, what merchants already pay and what they will pay for. Many small merchants resist percentage fees on low-margin sales but pay for working capital, faster settlement or tools that save time. I would test pricing in interviews with a commitment (a signed letter of intent) and compare the fee against local alternatives, such as cash on delivery costs. I would also check whether regulation limits the fee or requires disclosure. The first cohort's unit economics, with the cost of verification included, would tell me whether the model works at a small scale.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c09-05\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C09-05 · Hard · product design · PS · Apple</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Launch a mobile wallet in a market where card use is low. (Use India as the worked case.)</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Build a wallet where users can store money and pay at shops,&quot; with no analysis of what people use instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Tests the premise by asking what people use in place of cards, identifies the rival, chooses a segment and designs around regulation and trust.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sees that a free interoperable rail changes what a wallet can offer, competes on what a device maker can uniquely provide (security, setup, integration across devices, privacy) and not on a new balance, and handles revenue, regulation and distribution.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll test the premise first: &quot;card use is low&quot; does not mean people lack ways to pay. It means the way they pay is something else, and the answer depends on what. In India that is largely interoperable account-to-account transfers through the Unified Payments Interface (UPI), scanned from a QR code or approved in a payment app <code>Public</code> <code>[V: reference summaries of NPCI]</code>. Many different apps connect to the same rail, and merchant payments from bank accounts have carried no merchant fee by policy since 2020, with a 0.4% merchant discount rate (capped at ₹300) on merchant payments above ₹2,000 from 15 October 2026 <code>[V: merchant-fee FAQs of 15 September 2026, as reproduced by FinTech BizNews]</code>. So the rival is not cards; it is free, interoperable and already habitual. A new wallet with a stored balance of its own has to beat that, and it has no natural advantage.</p>\n<p><em>Goal and company.</em> I'll assume a device maker, so the setting is a phone, a watch and the services around them, and the goal is that the device becomes the way people pay, which strengthens the whole ecosystem. I know this only from public information, and nothing here describes any existing product.</p>\n<p><em>Segment.</em> By behavior: urban smartphone owners who pay in shops several times a day; people who have a bank account but no card; people new to digital payments in smaller towns. I choose the first, because the frequency is high, they have capable devices and the habit is established, so the switching question is clear. I give up first-time digital payers, who need a different, assisted design, and I'd treat them as a later segment.</p>\n<p><em>Constraint stack.</em> Payments bind: the dominant rail decides everything. Trust follows: people entrust money to an app, so the app's identity, security and dispute route matter. Regulation sets the order of work: payment intermediaries need authorization, and payment data must be stored in India (the Reserve Bank of India's 2018 directive) <code>Public</code> <code>[V: RBI FAQ, 2019; RBI payment aggregator directions, 2025]</code>, and general data-protection rules apply (the DPDP Act, in phases) <code>[V: law-firm summaries of the DPDP Rules]</code>.</p>\n<p><em>Options.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Idea</th>\n<th>Assessment</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. A closed wallet with its own balance</td>\n<td>Users load money; pays within the wallet</td>\n<td>Competes with free interoperable apps; needs licensing; no reason to switch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. A front end to the interoperable rail</td>\n<td>The wallet lets users pay from their bank accounts through the rail, with the device's strengths</td>\n<td>Fits the market; differentiates on trust, ease and security</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. A pass wallet for tickets, transit and loyalty cards</td>\n<td>Not payments; passes and identity</td>\n<td>Lower regulatory burden; a smaller first step</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Stay out of payments</td>\n<td>Focus on identity and passes</td>\n<td>Safe and passive</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>What a device maker adds.</em> On-device authentication (a fingerprint or face check), secure storage of credentials, a quick setup that finds the user's existing bank account, a tap or a scan that works the same on a phone and a watch, and a privacy stance (what stays on the device and what the company never sees). These are reasons to prefer the device's wallet that do not require a new balance.</p>\n<p><em>Recommendation.</em> I recommend B with C: a wallet that connects to the interoperable rail through an authorized app provider and a partnership with banks, plus passes for transit and tickets as a second use. It gives up the control of a closed wallet and a revenue stream from float, and gains fit with how people pay. Revenue is indirect (a stronger ecosystem) and perhaps from services, since a fee per payment is limited on this rail (none up to 2,000 rupees; 0.4% above that from 15 October 2026) <code>[V: merchant-fee FAQs of 15 September 2026, as reproduced by FinTech BizNews]</code>. Distribution: bank partnerships and merchant acceptance through QR codes that merchants already display. Next I'd add the watch and assisted setup for first-time users.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of eligible users who complete setup and make a first payment within seven days. Input: the share of setups that succeed on the first try. Guardrails: payment success rate, fraud losses and time to resolve a dispute. Counter metric: users who set the wallet up and go back to their previous payment app within a month, which shows whether it adds anything.</p>\n<p><em>Risks and limits.</em> Regulation could delay the start, so authorization and data storage begin first. Trust: a failed payment with no clear dispute route loses a user for good. I would not launch without a dispute route and clear refund rules. I'd change my plan if research showed that the habit was so strong, and the free apps so good, that setup alone would not move people; then C becomes the product.</p>\n<p><em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Public</code> that Apple publishes a privacy position and designs hardware and software together, and <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that wallet and payments prompts test local-rail understanding and privacy <code>[V: Apple privacy pages and integrated hardware, software and services; the prompt descriptions are candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any existing Apple product or its availability in any country.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What can a device maker offer that an existing payments rail and its apps do not? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Not a new balance or a new rail; that would compete where the free rail is already strong. What it can offer is what only a device maker controls: secure hardware that makes payment approval easy and safe, a fast setup that links an existing bank account in a minute, integration across the user's phone, watch and computer, and privacy features such as not sharing the full account number with the merchant. The measures are setup completion, repeat use at a merchant within 30 days and fraud rate, with a guardrail on failed payments. I would verify these capabilities against the rail's rules before promising them.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would the wallet earn revenue and handle regulation and distribution? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Revenue cannot rely on a fee on a rail that is free for users, so it would come from adjacent value: device sales and retention, paid services, or merchant-side services where the rules allow. I would check what regulation says about who may charge what, who must hold a license and where payment data must be stored <code>[V: RBI payment aggregator directions, 2025; RBI storage directive FAQ]</code>. Distribution is the strength of a device maker: pre-installed on the device and tied to account setup. The risk is a regulator treating a gatekeeper's wallet differently, so I would plan to work with the rail's operators and publish how the wallet interoperates.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c09-06\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C09-06 · Hard · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Design a short-term credit feature for delivery workers whose income is irregular. How do you avoid harming them?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;A quick loan with a clear interest rate,&quot; with no analysis of harm.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies the real need, designs a product that fits irregular income, and names the risk of a debt cycle with design responses.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Sets a written limit and a stop rule, measures repeat borrowing as a harm signal, protects data and consent, and notes the conflict of interest in a platform lending to its own workers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'll start with the check (FW-C09-07), because for credit the harm question shapes the product. The user is described by what products lack for them: delivery workers who are paid per job, whose earnings vary week to week while costs (fuel, repairs, a phone bill) come on fixed dates. The need is bridging a gap of a few days. Existing options are informal lenders or loans with high fees and fixed monthly repayments that do not fit irregular income.</p>\n<p><em>Design.</em> An advance against earnings already scheduled to arrive, repaid automatically from those earnings, with a flat, stated fee and no compounding interest. A cap: an advance may be at most a set share of average weekly earnings (say 30%, invented). No rollover: an advance cannot be extended or refinanced, and a new one cannot be taken until the last is repaid. The price is shown in the local language before the person agrees, as a single figure and in the effective annual cost, with a clear way to say no. Support is by voice as well as text.</p>\n<p><em>Harm signals.</em> The main risk is a debt cycle: a worker takes an advance every week because the last one left a gap. I'd measure the repeat-advance rate, the share of users who take a new advance within seven days of repaying, and treat a high rate as a sign that the product is creating dependence, not bridging a gap.</p>\n<p><em>Metrics.</em> Primary: the share of users who repay on time and do not take another advance within seven days. Guardrails: the effective cost of the fee, complaints, and the share of earnings taken by repayment. Counter metrics: the repeat-advance rate and the share of users who use it every week. Stop rule: if more than 25% of users take an advance in each of eight consecutive weeks (invented), I'd stop growth and redesign, since that pattern signals dependence.</p>\n<p><em>Conflict of interest and data.</em> A platform that employs the worker and lends to the worker has more power than an ordinary lender. Repayment from earnings makes repayment safe for the company and can leave the worker with too little, so the repayment share is capped. The platform's earnings data is used to decide the limit, and the worker must understand and agree to that use; the data is not sold or used for other purposes.</p>\n<p><em>Regulation.</em> Lending usually needs authorization and consumer-protection compliance, and details vary by country <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>, so I'd involve legal teams first or partner with a licensed lender.</p>\n<p><em>My limit.</em> I would not launch credit with fees that trap people in repeated borrowing, with hidden or rolling charges, or with collection methods that shame or threaten, even if that version grew fastest. I'd change my plan if the first cohort showed that most advances were repaid and not repeated; I'd widen the cap carefully, and if the opposite, I'd not launch at scale.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What written limit and stop rule would you set? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Limits in advance: an advance no larger than a stated share of the worker's recent weekly earnings (say 30%), a flat fee that is clear before acceptance with no compounding, and repayment taken as a percentage of future earnings, so a slow week means a smaller repayment. The stop rule: if repeat borrowing (the same worker advancing in more than half of the last eight weeks) exceeds a set share, say 15% of users, or if more than 3% of advances are repaid late, pause expansion and review the product. The rules are written before launch, and changes need sign-off by someone outside the team that earns from the product.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you handle the conflict of interest when a platform lends to its own workers? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By naming it and constraining it. The platform controls the workers' income and could pressure them through the loan, so I would separate decisions: workers cannot be penalized in work allocation for borrowing or not, advance decisions use limited data and not the worker's behavior on the platform beyond earnings, and the data is not used for other purposes. I would give workers an independent complaint route, publish the fees and outcomes, and review the product with an outside adviser. If these protections cannot be met, a partnership with a regulated lender may be better than lending directly.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c09-07\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C09-07 · Hard · product design · PS</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your product succeeded in its home market and failed in the first country it entered. You have two weeks to write a post-mortem. What do you do?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Conclude that the market was not ready and list lessons.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates assumptions from evidence, finds which dimension broke, uses funnel data and interviews, and proposes a decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Compares the new market's funnel step by step with the home one, includes people who left, tests alternative explanations, sets criteria for another attempt or an exit, and avoids blame.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd treat it as a diagnosis with a decision at the end, and I'd refuse the conclusion &quot;the market was not ready&quot; until the evidence earns it. The product has a funnel in both markets, so the first move is to compare them.</p>\n<p><em>Week 1: evidence.</em> Day 1 and 2: funnel by step for the new market and the home market, split by device, connection and channel, to find where the new market loses people (acquisition, sign-up, first use, payment, return). Day 2 to 4: the six dimensions as a checklist of what we assumed against what was true: users, infrastructure, regulation, competition, distribution and price. Day 3 to 5: interviews with ten people who installed and left, and five who stayed; calls with the local team and any partners; support tickets by theme. I'd include the people who left, because the success stories are not where the answer is.</p>\n<p><em>Example of what the evidence might show (invented).</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Assumption</th>\n<th>Evidence</th>\n<th>Verdict</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Users have a personal phone</td>\n<td>8 of 12 interviewees shared a phone</td>\n<td>Wrong; shared use</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Card payment works</td>\n<td>60% of payment attempts failed or were abandoned at the card step</td>\n<td>Wrong; payments</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Paid ads will bring users at home-market cost</td>\n<td>Cost per install was three times higher and retention was lower</td>\n<td>Wrong; distribution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Translation is enough</td>\n<td>Support tickets about language were 5% of the total</td>\n<td>Mostly right</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Alternative explanations.</em> A single cause is unusual. I'd ask whether launch timing, a competitor's move, or a technical fault (a payment provider outage) explains part of it, and whether the home market's success depended on conditions that do not exist here (for example, a network effect that needs density).</p>\n<p><em>Week 2: synthesis and decision.</em> A short decision memo (FW-C02-06): what happened, which assumptions failed and what the evidence is, what we do not know, the options, and a recommendation. Options: iterate in the same market with specific fixes, narrow to one city or segment, change the model (a partner for distribution or a local payment method), or exit. For each, I'd write the condition that would make it right, such as &quot;continue only if payment success reaches 85% and week-four retention reaches 20% within a quarter (invented).&quot;</p>\n<p><em>Blame and learning.</em> I'd write the post-mortem about decisions and information, not about people, and I'd hold a pre-mortem (FW-C02-05) for any second attempt: assume it failed in a year and ask why. I'd also record what worked, since a failed entry usually contains a part that did. I'd change my recommendation if the funnel showed that most of the loss happened before the product was even seen, in which case the question is distribution and not the product.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How do you compare the new market's funnel with the home market's? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Step by step, with the same definitions, split by device, connection and channel. Acquisition, sign-up, first use, payment and return are the steps; I would find where the new market loses people compared with home: for example 60% of sign-ups complete the first task at home against 25% in the new market, concentrated on low-end phones. Then I would interview people who left at that step, and not only active users. This turns &quot;the market was not ready&quot; into a specific, testable finding: a step, a segment and a cause, such as slow load times or an unavailable payment method.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What criteria would you set for another attempt or an exit? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Written in the post-mortem, with numbers. For another attempt: the failure step is identified with evidence, a fix is feasible within a stated budget and time, and a small test can show a result, for example reaching a first-week completion of 50% in a pilot of 5,000 users within three months. For an exit: no segment shows at least a threshold of retention, the cost to fix exceeds the expected return, or the license or payment route is unavailable. The post-mortem avoids blame by describing decisions in light of what was known then and what the team would do differently now.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c09-12-exercises\"></span>\n<h3>12. Exercises</h3>\n<p>Ten exercises. Each has a model solution and an alternative. Figures are assumptions unless marked.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c09-01\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C09-01 · The six-dimension scan</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A food-delivery app from a large, card-based market plans to launch in a city of five million where, as you assume, many addresses are landmark-based, cash on delivery is common, and couriers ride motorbikes. Fill the market-difference scan and mark the two differences that matter most.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You have two minutes in an interview to say what differs.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Use the assumptions in the problem, and add your own labeled ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> One line per dimension.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use FW-C09-02: assumption, difference from home, what to verify, and a mark on the decisive ones.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Users: customers who order for families and workplaces; verify with ten interviews. Infrastructure: landmark addresses, phones of mid-range quality, variable connections; at home an address is a street number. Regulation: courier and food-safety licensing; verify first. Competition: restaurants' own delivery and phone orders; the rival is the phone call. Distribution: restaurant partnerships and workplace referrals more than app-store advertising. Price: low order value; a delivery fee that is high relative to the meal. Decisive: addresses (the core flow depends on finding the customer) and payment (cash changes settlement and risk).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Mark regulation as decisive if a license is needed before any launch.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Marking two keeps the answer focused and risks leaving out a decisive dimension; say what you would check to be sure.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c09-02\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C09-02 · Find the binding constraint</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A language-learning app is launching in a market where, as you learn: 70% of target users share a phone with family; data plans are small; phones have limited storage; people are comfortable reading; payment is by prepaid top-up. Which constraint binds, and what is the first design move?</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The app currently streams video lessons and requires a personal account.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the stack (FW-C09-03): trace the flow (find, sign up, learn, pay, return) and mark where each constraint stops it.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Shared use stops sign-up and progress tracking (a personal account assumes one person); data and storage stop learning (streaming video uses the plan; storage is limited). Literacy is not binding. Payment can be handled by prepaid top-up. The binding constraints are shared use and data. First moves: profiles on one device with a simple code, and lessons that download over Wi-Fi in a small file size.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Choose data cost as the single binding constraint, since without it no lesson plays, and treat shared use as the next problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A design for the tightest case can reduce quality for people with ample data; allow a richer path where bandwidth exists.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c09-03\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C09-03 · Translate, adapt or rethink?</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Classify each change as translate, adapt or rethink: (a) interface text in the local language; (b) displaying numbers with lakh and crore groupings; (c) a date format; (d) support for cash on delivery, with reconciliation of cash collected by couriers; (e) identifying users by phone number instead of email; (f) onboarding by a local agent instead of self-service; (g) replacing stock photos with local ones; (h) voice support in the local language; (i) a local legal entity and terms in the local language.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are scoping a launch.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> One word each and a line on the launch scope.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the ladder (FW-C09-05).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) Translate. (b) Adapt. (c) Adapt. (d) Rethink: the home model assumed card payment. (e) Adapt, or rethink if email is rarely used and the whole sign-up flow rests on it. (f) Rethink. (g) Adapt. (h) Adapt (a support channel). (i) Adapt, and a prerequisite. Launch scope: translate and adapt everything on the critical path (sign-up, ordering, payment, support), do (d) and (f) if cash and trust are binding, and defer (g).</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Treat (e) and (h) as rethink if most users cannot or will not use text.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Deeper localization costs time and delays learning; shallow localization can break trust at the first payment.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c09-04\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C09-04 · Low-revenue economics</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A savings app earns $0.50 per active user per month, costs $0.20 a month to serve, and loses 10% of users each month. Acquiring a user through advertising costs $2.50; through a partner network, $1.00. Compute margin, lifetime, lifetime margin, payback and the ratio of lifetime margin to acquisition cost for each channel. Then repeat for 15% churn.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The team must pick a first channel.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> As above.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Show each step.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use FW-C09-06.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Margin = $0.50 − $0.20 = $0.30. Lifetime = 1 ÷ 0.10 = 10 months. Lifetime margin = $0.30 × 10 = $3.00. Advertising: payback $2.50 ÷ $0.30 = 8.3 months; ratio $3.00 ÷ $2.50 = 1.2. Partner: payback $1.00 ÷ $0.30 = 3.3 months; ratio 3.0. At 15% churn: lifetime = 1 ÷ 0.15 = 6.7 months; lifetime margin = $2.00; advertising ratio $2.00 ÷ $2.50 = 0.8 (loses money); partner ratio 2.0. The advertising route is fragile: a small rise in churn turns it from marginal to unprofitable, and the partner route stays positive.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Compare payback with the expected lifetime directly: advertising pays back in 8.3 of 10 months at 10% churn, and in 8.3 of 6.7 months at 15%, so it never pays back.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Churn may differ by channel (users from partners may stay longer), which would widen the gap, and the numbers should be checked with a pilot.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c09-05\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C09-05 · A discovery plan for street vendors</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> You want to learn whether street vendors in a city you do not know would accept digital payments. You have two weeks and a small budget. Write the plan using FW-C09-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The company has no presence in the city.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Workarounds, proxies, fieldwork with local partners, a cheap test, and bias corrections.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Workarounds: how vendors take payment today (cash, a customer's transfer to a personal account, a regular customer's tab), and what it costs them (change, theft, time). Proxies: how they pay suppliers, whether they keep a phone and use it for transfers, how they handle small sums. Fieldwork: a local researcher spends a day at three markets with 12 vendors, watching a busy hour. Cheap test: a printed QR code for the vendor's bank account, with a local partner recording how many customers use it over a week. Biases: politeness (vendors may say yes to a visitor), the busy hour (a vendor cannot talk during it), and selecting only vendors who speak to visitors. Decision rule: proceed if at least a quarter of customers at a vendor use the code within a week (invented) and vendors report a real cost of cash handling.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Start with suppliers and customers: ask what stops them from paying vendors digitally.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A QR test with a personal account means vendors take payment through an existing rail and says little about a new product; it tests demand for digital payment, which is the first question.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c09-06\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C09-06 · Fix the research design</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A team surveys a new market by calling randomly chosen phone numbers on weekday afternoons with an interviewer from the capital city, asking, &quot;Would you use a savings app if it were available?&quot; Ninety percent say yes. Name four problems and propose a better design.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The team plans to build on this result.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> The 90% figure.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the biases in C09.2 and FW-C09-04.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> Problems: sampling (people without a phone, people who do not answer during working hours, and people who share a phone are missed); interviewer effect (a visitor from the capital invites polite answers); a hypothetical question (&quot;would you use,&quot; not &quot;when did you last&quot;); and the lack of a commitment (saying yes costs nothing). Better design: recruit in person at markets and workplaces; use local interviewers; ask about the last time the person saved and what they did with the money; observe a few cases; and offer a small, real commitment, such as signing up for a concierge deposit service, and count sign-ups.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Keep the phone survey but use it only to size what interviews found, with questions about behavior, and report the sample's limits.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> In-person work is slower and costlier than calling, and its results describe people you can actually learn from.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c09-07\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C09-07 · Apply the underserved-user check</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> A shopping app proposes &quot;buy now, pay later&quot; for low-income shoppers, with a late fee. Apply the check (FW-C09-07), decide, and state a limit.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Revenue would come mostly from late fees (invented: 70% of the feature's revenue).</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Late fees are 70% of the feature's revenue.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Five minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Go through access, dignity, exploitation, safety, consent and limit.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> <em>Access:</em> the feature widens access to goods for people without credit cards. <em>Dignity:</em> describe it by what it offers, not as help for the poor. <em>Exploitation:</em> a business model in which 70% of revenue comes from late fees profits when customers fail, which is the clearest warning; the incentive must not depend on lateness. <em>Safety:</em> repeated late fees can lead to debt and collections. <em>Consent:</em> the fee schedule must be shown before purchase, in plain language. Decision: proceed only with a different revenue model, such as a fee paid by the merchant for a larger sale, no late fees beyond a small cap, and credit limits tied to the person's ability to repay. <em>Limit:</em> I would not launch if the plan needed more than a small share of revenue from late fees. Stop rule: if more than 20% of users are late in two consecutive months (invented), pause growth.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Do not build it, and say that a product whose economics reward customer failure cannot be made safe by disclosures.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A safer model earns less per user and may grow slowly; the alternative can harm the people it claims to help.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c09-08\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C09-08 · Pick the product shape by payment environment</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> For a new wallet or checkout, choose a product shape in each environment: (A) cards are widely used; (B) interoperable account-to-account transfers are widely used and free for merchants; (C) mobile-money agents with cash-in and cash-out are the main way to move money.</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> The company is a device maker and a payments platform.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Two sentences each.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Find the dominant rail first, then decide what the product adds (C09.4).</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (A) Card-based wallet: tokenized cards, device authentication and tap to pay; compete on security and convenience. (B) A front end to the shared rail with the device's strengths (authentication, quick setup, a scan or tap that works on a phone and a watch); do not rely on a fee per payment, and compete on trust, ease and software for merchants. (C) Integrate with the mobile-money operator and its agents: flows that work on basic phones through menus, cash-out at agents, receipts in the local language, and a trust signal from the agent.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> In (B), start with passes and identity (tickets, transit) where there is no incumbent, and add payments later.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Each choice leans on the dominant rail, which brings reach and dependence on whoever runs it.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c09-09\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C09-09 · A timed full run</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Give a 25-minute answer to: &quot;Launch a fitness-tracking app in a country you do not know well.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> Self-scored; record yourself. Choose a country or describe a market and say your assumptions aloud.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> None. Label what you assume.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Use the short-version budget in section 4: one difference that matters, one constraint, one change per ladder level, one channel, one metric, one limit.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the full spine (FW-C09-01) and score yourself against the six behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> An outline of a strong answer. Clarify: an existing app with a free tier; goal: a first cohort that stays. Scan: the decisive differences are devices (many users have no wearable and mid-range phones) and trust and payment (a subscription needs a local payment method). Segment: office workers who walk and run at fixed times, as a context-based segment. Constraint: devices and battery (continuous location tracking drains a mid-range phone).</p>\n<p>Shape: translate; adapt units, local sports and festival-based challenges; rethink by tracking steps from the phone's motion sensor, with no wearable needed. Distribution: workplaces and a carrier bundle; economics: low revenue per user, so a sponsor-funded or B2B2C model may be the first source of revenue. Metric: users active in week four. Limit: no location data kept without consent; no health claims.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Choose a different segment (runners with wearables) and say you traded size for fit.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> A phone-only design reaches more people and measures less accurately than a wearable.</p>\n<span id=\"ex-c09-10\"></span>\n<h5>EX-C09-10 · Handling interventions</h5>\n<p><strong>Problem.</strong> Write a one- to two-sentence reply to each interviewer intervention: (a) &quot;What is different about this market?&quot; (b) &quot;How do you know that?&quot; (c) &quot;Who exactly is the user?&quot; (d) &quot;How will people find it?&quot; (e) &quot;Can this make money?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Context.</strong> You are partway through an answer about launching a ride-hailing service in a new city.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Use any assumptions you state.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Two minutes.</p>\n<p><strong>Expected approach.</strong> Use the table of interventions in section 4: each points to a missing step.</p>\n<p><strong>Solution.</strong> (a) &quot;Two things matter most: addresses are given by landmark, and most riders pay in cash, so finding the rider and collecting commission are both different from home.&quot; (b) &quot;I don't know it; it's an assumption from public information. I'd check it with a local researcher and a week of observation.&quot; (c) &quot;Office commuters who today bargain for informal taxis on a regular route, chosen for frequency and a repeated pain.&quot; (d) &quot;Drivers through in-person hubs and riders through workplace referrals; both build trust and avoid paying for advertising that few would see.&quot; (e) &quot;Per trip I'd expect a margin of about 14.50 on a fare of 150 (invented); it works if pickups succeed and fails if they do not, so pickup success is the number I'd watch.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative solution.</strong> Record yourself and check that each reply takes under 20 seconds and returns to the spine.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Short replies keep momentum and can hide nuance; offer more if asked.</p>\n<span id=\"c09-13-practice-protocol\"></span>\n<h3>13. Practice protocol</h3>\n<p><strong>Timed drills (three a week for four weeks).</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>Week 1: the market and the segment.</em> Take a prompt from the exercises or questions (IQ-C09-01, EX-C09-01, EX-C09-02) and spend ten minutes on the scan, the segment and a criterion. Say which differences are assumptions.</li>\n<li><em>Week 2: the constraint and the shape.</em> Use IQ-C09-02, IQ-C09-03, EX-C09-03 and EX-C09-05. Find the binding constraint, sort changes into translate, adapt and rethink, and write a first version as moments. Twelve minutes.</li>\n<li><em>Week 3: economics, regulation and ethics.</em> Use IQ-C09-04, IQ-C09-05, IQ-C09-06, EX-C09-04 and EX-C09-07. Sketch unit economics by channel, name a rule that sets the order of work, and write a limit. Fifteen minutes.</li>\n<li><em>Week 4: full rounds.</em> Run two 45-minute answers and one 25-minute answer (EX-C09-09) on markets you have not prepared, with a partner who uses the interventions in section 4.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>With a partner.</strong> Ask your partner to interrupt with &quot;How do you know that?&quot; and &quot;Who exactly is the user?&quot; at random points, and once with &quot;Can this make money?&quot; Practice answering in under a minute, and practice saying &quot;that is an assumption, and I'd check it by...&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Read before you drill.</strong> Read one public source on the market you will practice with (a central bank's payments statistics, a regulator's guidelines, a field report) so that your assumptions rest on something. Label every fact from it with a verify mark.</p>\n<p><strong>Record and review.</strong> Record every full run. Listen once for structure and once for wording. Count how many statements about the market were stated as facts, whether you gave a segment and a binding constraint, and whether you mentioned payments, trust and regulation.</p>\n<p><strong>Feedback checklist (yes, partly, no).</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Did I name what differs from the home market and mark assumptions as assumptions?</li>\n<li>Did I choose a segment inside the market by need and context?</li>\n<li>Did I find the binding constraint and design for it first?</li>\n<li>Did I sort changes into translate, adapt and rethink, and go beyond language?</li>\n<li>Did I say how people will find the product and why they would trust it?</li>\n<li>Did I sketch revenue, cost to serve, cost to acquire and churn?</li>\n<li>Did I name a regulatory requirement and a way users could be harmed, with a response?</li>\n<li>Did I state a limit on what I would not build?</li>\n<li>Did I avoid stereotypes and describe users by access and context?</li>\n<li>Did I finish with the two-sentence close from section 4?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer journal.</strong> For each drill record the date, the market, the segment, the binding constraint, the channel chosen, your score out of ten and the step you rushed. After eight drills, find the step you skip most (usually economics or the limit) and drill it alone for a week.</p>\n<span id=\"c09-14-implementation-playbook\"></span>\n<h3>14. Implementation playbook</h3>\n<p>Apply the chapter on real work over the next three weeks. Each deliverable doubles as an interview story, and it works whether or not your product is international: the same questions apply to any group of users whom the product serves poorly.</p>\n<p><strong>Days 1 to 3.</strong> Split one core funnel in your product by country, device type and connection speed, or by any segment you suspect is served poorly. Note the steps where one segment loses far more than the others, and list your guesses about why.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Test the product on a low-end device or a throttled connection for the core task. Write down every step that is slow, heavy or fails. Share the list with an engineer and a designer and ask which two matter most.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 1.</strong> Talk to a colleague or a user who knows a market or group you do not serve well. Ask about the last time they tried to do the product's task, and what they used instead. Write the workaround down.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 2.</strong> Fill the market-difference scan (FW-C09-02) for a country or group you might serve next, from public sources. Mark each fact as known, assumed or to verify, and bring it to a conversation with someone who knows the place.</p>\n<p><strong>Week 3.</strong> Sketch the unit economics (FW-C09-06) for one channel in your own product: revenue per user, cost to serve, cost to acquire, churn. Name the assumption you are least sure of and how you would check it.</p>\n<p><strong>Deliverables that become stories:</strong> the segmented funnel (a time you found who the product failed and why), the low-end device test (a time you found a constraint before users did), and the scan or economics sketch (a time you planned for conditions you could not see). Keep the numbers real, and keep what you did separate from what the team did.</p>\n<span id=\"c09-15-key-takeaways-and-further-reading\"></span>\n<h3>15. Key takeaways and further reading</h3>\n<span id=\"c09-key-takeaways\"></span>\n<h4>Key takeaways</h4>\n<ol>\n<li>A new market changes the conditions a product relies on: users, infrastructure, regulation, competition, distribution and price. Name what differs, mark what you assume, and never treat a region as one market.</li>\n<li>Find evidence without an existing market by studying today's workarounds, using proxies and local researchers, and testing behavior in one place, with the biases of cross-cultural research in mind.</li>\n<li>Find the binding constraint (connectivity, devices, payments, trust, literacy, shared use) and design for it first; go beyond translation to adapt and, where the home model fails, rethink.</li>\n<li>Distribution and economics are part of the product: at low revenue per user, the cost to acquire and serve and the rate of churn decide whether the business works.</li>\n<li>Design for underserved users by describing what products lack for them, check for exploitation and consent, and state a limit on what you would not build.</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c09-further-reading\"></span>\n<h4>Further reading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Nick Hughes and Susie Lonie, &quot;M-PESA: Mobile Money for the 'Unbanked': Turning Cellphones into 24-Hour Tellers in Kenya,&quot; <em>Innovations: Technology, Governance, Globalization</em> 2(1-2), 63 to 81 (MIT Press, 2007). The primary source for CS-C09-02.</li>\n<li>Jan Chipchase, <em>The Field Study Handbook</em> (2017), and Jan Chipchase and Simon Steinhardt, <em>Hidden in Plain Sight</em> (2013). Practical guides to fieldwork in unfamiliar places.</li>\n<li>Abhijit V. Banerjee and Esther Duflo, <em>Poor Economics</em> (2011). A research-based view of how people on low incomes make decisions, useful against stereotypes.</li>\n<li>Daryl Collins, Jonathan Morduch, Stuart Rutherford and Orlanda Ruthven, <em>Portfolios of the Poor</em> (2009). How households with low incomes manage money, from financial diaries.</li>\n<li>Nandan Nilekani and Viral Shah, <em>Rebooting India</em> (2015). Background on India's digital public infrastructure and the thinking behind its payment systems <code>[V: Allen Lane (Penguin India), November 2015, ISBN 9780670087891; about technology-led public institutions built on Aadhaar]</code>.</li>\n<li>Reserve Bank of India, &quot;Storage of Payment System Data&quot; (circular of 6 April 2018), and National Payments Corporation of India, UPI product statistics. Primary sources for the regional facts in this chapter <code>[V: RBI circular of 6 April 2018, via RBI FAQ]</code>.</li>\n<li>Kat Holmes, <em>Mismatch: How Inclusion Shapes Design</em> (2018), cited in C07, for inclusive design applied to groups that products leave out.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c10\"></span>\n<h2>C10 — UX and Product Quality</h2>\n<span id=\"c10-1-what-interviewers-test\"></span>\n<h3>1. What interviewers test</h3>\n<p>Product interviews rarely ask for a design critique in the vocabulary of design, and they reward it when it appears. A candidate who says &quot;this screen has no error state, and the label says 'Error 4021', which tells the user nothing about what to do&quot; is easier to trust than one who says &quot;it feels clunky.&quot; The interviewer is not looking for a designer. They are looking for a PM who can see why an interface fails, name the problem precisely, weigh the quality tradeoffs a team actually faces, notice when a design manipulates people, and work well with the people who design.</p>\n<p><strong>Where it shows up.</strong> <code>Inferred</code> from the round structures candidates commonly report: critique, &quot;improve this screen&quot; and design prompts at Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon, Apple and others, where the interviewer listens for UX reasoning inside a product-sense answer, and behavioral questions about working with designers and handling disagreements <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. This chapter supplies the vocabulary that C11 (critique and teardown) uses.</p>\n<p><strong>What is scored.</strong> Whether your reasoning about interfaces is specific and user-centered <code>Inferred</code>: do you walk the flow as a user, name the principle at stake, consider the states other than the happy path, think of people who use the product differently from you, propose a way to test, and know where to spend craft and where not to.</p>\n<p>Six observable behaviors:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>You walk a flow from the user's task, not from the screen.</li>\n<li>You name the problem with a principle (a heuristic, an affordance, a cognitive-load issue) and say what it costs the user.</li>\n<li>You check the states beyond the ideal: empty, loading, error, offline, permission denied.</li>\n<li>You consider accessibility and people who differ from you (ability, language, device, situation).</li>\n<li>You decide how much quality a feature deserves and say why.</li>\n<li>You spot manipulative patterns, and you say what you would do instead.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The failure this prevents.</strong> Six patterns weaken UX answers: &quot;it feels clunky&quot; with no principle, a critique of visual taste only, designing only the ideal path, ignoring accessibility, polishing everything equally (or nothing), and optimizing a number by making the interface pushier.</p>\n<span id=\"c10-2-the-concept-from-first-principles\"></span>\n<h3>2. The concept from first principles</h3>\n<span id=\"c10-c10-1-the-pm-s-role-in-ux\"></span>\n<h4>C10.1 The PM's role in UX</h4>\n";
